WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:02.589
Hey there, and thanks for stopping by!

00:00:02.589 --> 00:00:03.662
My name&nbsp;is Bradley Knapp,

00:00:03.662 --> 00:00:06.194
and I'm one of the product managers here at IBM Cloud,

00:00:06.194 --> 00:00:09.144
and the question&nbsp;that we're trying to help you answer today is:

00:00:09.144 --> 00:00:15.251
what is the difference between a bare metal with&nbsp;
a hypervisor on it and a dedicated host?

00:00:15.251 --> 00:00:17.985
because the underlying concept is pretty similar,

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:20.540
but the&nbsp;exact use-cases are going to be very different.

00:00:20.742 --> 00:00:24.863
Both bare metals with hypervisors and&nbsp;
dedicated hosts have advantages. 

00:00:24.863 --> 00:00:26.619
They also have some disadvantages,

00:00:26.619 --> 00:00:30.507
and so selecting&nbsp;the right one to solve your workload needs

00:00:30.507 --> 00:00:34.885
is going to be essential and critical in solving&nbsp;your problems,

00:00:34.885 --> 00:00:38.278
because even though they do look and act and feel kind of the same,

00:00:38.278 --> 00:00:42.947
those added&nbsp;benefits, those added drawbacks, they're going to make the decision for you.

00:00:42.947 --> 00:00:48.099
And so, if we start&nbsp;from the very beginning, we need to think about a cloud compute host,

00:00:48.099 --> 00:00:53.433
because that's the common&nbsp;element that every compute offering in cloud has.

00:00:53.433 --> 00:00:56.269
So, on this side we're going to have our&nbsp;bare metal with the hypervisor.

00:00:56.269 --> 00:00:58.643
On this side we're going to have our dedicated host.

00:00:58.643 --> 00:01:02.212
And so,&nbsp;let's start out with our actual host.

00:01:02.212 --> 00:01:06.996
This is the physical compute host with the sockets and the RAM&nbsp;and the processors

00:01:06.996 --> 00:01:10.649
in the cloud data center that you build on top of.

00:01:10.649 --> 00:01:16.322
So, we're going to start&nbsp;with our boxes, with either a bare metal with a hypervisor or dedicated host.

00:01:16.322 --> 00:01:19.972
Those boxes are&nbsp;the same. The underpinning compute is the same.

00:01:20.238 --> 00:01:24.290
The difference is who runs the hypervisor.

00:01:24.290 --> 00:01:26.557
On&nbsp;bare metal with the hypervisor,

00:01:26.557 --> 00:01:30.967
(this is going to be our hypervisor layer up here, HV),

00:01:30.967 --> 00:01:34.935
that&nbsp;hypervisor is run by you, it's run by the client.

00:01:34.935 --> 00:01:40.263
It gives you all of the control, all of the&nbsp;tweaking, that you're used to having on-  premises,

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:46.000
and so you really can get in and manipulate that&nbsp;
hypervisor to make the box do whatever you want.
&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:53.318
Over on our dedicated host&nbsp;side, we again have that hypervisor layer that&nbsp;&nbsp;stays on top, 

00:01:53.318 --> 00:01:55.844
but the cloud provider runs this one.

00:01:55.844 --> 00:02:00.350
And so we're going to apply all of our knowledge, all of our best practices, to this hypervisor.

00:02:00.350 --> 00:02:03.161
We're going to take that complexity away from you.

00:02:03.161 --> 00:02:07.854
We're going to take care of that, so that you&nbsp;
can focus just on the virtual server instances (VSI).

00:02:07.854 --> 00:02:11.137
And so, when it comes time to deploy those VSIs

00:02:11.137 --> 00:02:15.810
on&nbsp;the dedicated host, you just tell us what kind of VSIs and how many you want,

00:02:15.810 --> 00:02:19.289
and then we schedule&nbsp;them against the hypervisor and place them on it for you.

00:02:19.289 --> 00:02:22.727
So, you're going to have virtual&nbsp;server 1, virtual server 2,

00:02:22.727 --> 00:02:28.061
virtual server 3, so on and so forth, until you fill up the&nbsp;box.

00:02:28.061 --> 00:02:32.103
On the bare metal with the hypervisor side, slightly different concept

00:02:32.103 --> 00:02:36.489
because, rather&nbsp;than you telling the cloud provider, "I would like to schedule these",

00:02:36.489 --> 00:02:38.525
you're going to&nbsp;take care of all of this yourself.

00:02:38.525 --> 00:02:45.059
You're going to interact with that hypervisor layer directly&nbsp;
to schedule those virtual servers yourself.

00:02:45.059 --> 00:02:47.501
Now, why would you ever want to do this?

00:02:47.501 --> 00:02:50.964
Well, because&nbsp;it does allow you for greater efficiency.

00:02:50.964 --> 00:02:56.623
If you want to do what's called "over-commit", where&nbsp;
you're going to have a single processor thread,

00:02:56.623 --> 00:03:01.621
but you're going to commit that to several&nbsp;
different virtual servers to using pooling,

00:03:01.621 --> 00:03:06.768
so that you can get greater compute&nbsp;capacity by taking advantage of this host,

00:03:06.768 --> 00:03:09.822
you can do that on a bare metal with a hypervisor.

00:03:09.822 --> 00:03:11.207
On the dedicated host,

00:03:11.207 --> 00:03:16.765
we don't have the ability to allow you to control what level of over-commit&nbsp;there is,

00:03:16.765 --> 00:03:20.736
but when you're on the hypervisor, you make all those decisions yourself.

00:03:20.736 --> 00:03:23.917
Also, because&nbsp;you have access to this hypervisor,

00:03:23.917 --> 00:03:27.322
it allows you to do a level of monitoring and a level of logging

00:03:27.322 --> 00:03:31.611
that, generally speaking, cloud providers don't make available on dedicated hosts.

00:03:31.611 --> 00:03:37.168
It also&nbsp;allows you to back up your virtual machines directly through that hypervisor layer,

00:03:37.168 --> 00:03:39.757
plugging&nbsp;into the API of that hypervisor.

00:03:39.757 --> 00:03:42.695
Whereas, on a dedicated host, if you want to back up a virtual&nbsp;server,

00:03:42.695 --> 00:03:45.853
you have to use the cloud provider's APIs.

00:03:45.853 --> 00:03:48.954
So, again with the bare metal with the&nbsp;hypervisor on it,

00:03:48.954 --> 00:03:53.766
the big benefit is that you have granular control of everything you want to do.

00:03:53.766 --> 00:03:58.797
You&nbsp;can over-commit VCPUs, you can over commit RAM, you can over schedule the box

00:03:58.797 --> 00:04:04.175
so, if it normally&nbsp;would only hold 5 VSIs, you can make the decision to put 10 on it.

00:04:04.175 --> 00:04:08.360
Because you know your&nbsp;workload best, and you can make those decisions.

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:14.240
On the dedicated host, because the cloud provider&nbsp;
is running that hypervisor just like it does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:18.705
any other hypervisor in the fleet, we're going to&nbsp;
abstract some of that control away,

00:04:18.705 --> 00:04:20.984
we're going to take care of that for you.

00:04:20.984 --> 00:04:22.891
But the benefit that&nbsp;goes along with it

00:04:22.891 --> 00:04:25.546
is it means that you don't have the added complexity

00:04:25.546 --> 00:04:29.190
that comes along with&nbsp;managing that bare metal server on your side.

00:04:29.190 --> 00:04:34.544
So, it really does matter, and the decision&nbsp;point is: how much control do you want?

00:04:34.544 --> 00:04:39.542
How much control are you willing to give up, and how&nbsp;
much simplicity do you want?

00:04:39.542 --> 00:04:44.624
Because there's no doubt a dedicated host is a simpler model for you&nbsp;to run

00:04:44.624 --> 00:04:46.789
than a bare metal with a hypervisor is,

00:04:46.789 --> 00:04:50.200
but with that simplicity comes a loss of control

00:04:50.200 --> 00:04:55.666
and a loss of the ability to go in and tinker with each of those individual settings.

00:04:56.319 --> 00:04:58.603
Thank you&nbsp;so much for your time today.

00:04:58.603 --> 00:05:01.659
If you have any questions, please drop us a line below.

00:05:01.659 --> 00:05:04.641
If you&nbsp;want to see more videos like this in the future,

00:05:04.641 --> 00:05:07.347
please do like and subscribe and let us know.

